The difference between *hurt* and *grievous hurt* is defined under Section 116 of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023.

**Hurt** is defined as bodily pain, disease, or infirmity caused to any person (Section 114 of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023).

**Grievous hurt**, on the other hand, refers to specific kinds of injury that are more severe. According to Section 116, the following kinds of hurt are designated as *grievous*:

(a) Emasculation;  
(b) Permanent privation of the sight of either eye;  
(c) Permanent privation of the hearing of either ear;  
(d) Privation of any member or joint;  
(e) Destruction or permanent impairing of the powers of any member or joint;  
(f) Permanent disfiguration of the head or face;  
(g) Fracture or dislocation of a bone or tooth;  
(h) Any hurt which endangers life or which causes the sufferer to be during the space of fifteen days in severe bodily pain, or unable to follow his ordinary pursuits.

Thus, while *hurt* is a broader term covering any bodily pain or illness, *grievous hurt* is a specific, more serious form of injury as listed above, and it carries more severe legal consequences under the law.

A chargesheet is a formal document prepared by the prosecutor (or the investigating officer) after collecting evidence in a criminal case, which outlines the allegations made against the accused and the evidence that supports those allegations. It is not a charge in the legal sense, but rather a detailed record of the case that is submitted to the court for trial.

Under Section 249 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023, when the accused appears before the court, the prosecutor is required to open the case by describing the charge brought against the accused and stating by what evidence he proposes to prove the guilt of the accused. This description forms part of the chargesheet.

The chargesheet typically includes:
- The name of the accused;
- The offence(s) charged;
- The time, place, and person (if any) involved;
- A detailed statement of the facts of the case;
- The evidence collected, including witness statements, documents, and other material;
- The legal provisions under which the accused is charged (as required under Section 234 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023).

It is important to note that the chargesheet is not a charge in the sense of a formal accusation, but rather a comprehensive summary of the case that the prosecution intends to prove. The accused is entitled to be informed of the nature and particulars of the charge, as required under Section 234 and Section 235 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023.

The chargesheet is submitted to the court before the trial begins and serves as the foundation upon which the trial proceeds. It may also include details about the manner in which the offence was committed, as required under Section 236 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023, where necessary.

In summary, a chargesheet is a formal, evidence-based document that sets out the allegations and supporting facts in a criminal case, prepared by the prosecution and submitted to the court for trial.

A First Information Report (FIR) is not explicitly defined in the provided legal provisions as a standalone document with a fixed, enumerated content. However, under **Section 193(3)(b)** of the *Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023*, the police officer in charge of a police station is required to include in the report submitted to a Magistrate the **nature of the information** relating to the commission of an offence.

Additionally, **Section 193(3)(c)** requires the report to include the **names of the persons who appear to be acquainted with the circumstances of the case**.

While the FIR itself is not fully detailed in the provided provisions, the **report submitted under Section 193(3)** — which is based on the information recorded in the FIR — must contain:

- The **nature of the information** (i.e., the facts of the alleged offence);
- The **names of persons acquainted with the circumstances** of the case.

Furthermore, **Section 193(3)(i)** mandates that where the investigation relates to an offence under sections 64, 65, 66, 67, 68, 70, or 71 of the *Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023*, the report must include the **report of medical examination of the woman**, if applicable.

Also, **Section 193(3)(f)** requires the report to state whether the accused has been arrested, released on bond or bail, or forwarded in custody under section 190.

Thus, while the exact content of the FIR is not fully specified in the provided provisions, the **report submitted under Section 193(3)** — which is based on the FIR — must contain:

- The nature of the information;
- Names of persons acquainted with the circumstances;
- Whether any offence appears to have been committed and by whom;
- Whether the accused has been arrested, released on bond, or forwarded in custody;
- Whether a medical examination report has been attached (in cases of sexual offences).

**Official guidance** indicates that when filing an FIR, the complainant should provide:
- A recent photograph;
- Physical description of the missing or victim;
- Last seen location and time;
- Any devices or accounts (e.g., phone number, social media) used by the person.

This information supports the investigation and helps in the preparation of the FIR and subsequent report under Section 193.

In summary, based on the provided law, a First Information Report is not fully detailed, but the **report submitted under Section 193(3)** of the *Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023* must contain the nature of the information, names of persons acquainted with the circumstances, and details of the accused’s status (arrest, bail, custody), among other elements. The FIR itself is the initial record of the information, and its content is reflected in the formal police report under Section 193.

No, a confession made to a police officer is **not admissible in court** unless it meets specific legal requirements under the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam, 2023.

According to **Section 23 of the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam, 2023**, no confession made to a police officer shall be proved against a person accused of any offence. This means that a confession given directly to a police officer cannot be used as evidence in court.

However, there is an exception under the **same section**:

> "Provided that when any fact is deposed to as discovered in consequence of information received from a person accused of any offence, in the custody of a police officer, so much of such information, whether it amounts to a confession or not, as relates distinctly to the fact discovered, may be proved."

This means that only the **specific fact** discovered from the accused (not the entire confession) may be admitted if it is directly linked to the offence and is part of information received during police custody.

Furthermore, **Section 183 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023** provides that a confession or statement must be recorded **by a Magistrate**, not by a police officer. The Magistrate must:

- Explain to the accused that he is not bound to make a confession;
- Ensure the confession is made voluntarily;
- Record it in the presence of the accused and read it back to him;
- Have it signed by the accused;
- Make a formal memorandum stating that the confession was voluntary and properly explained.

Only such a confession recorded by a Magistrate is admissible in court.

Additionally, **Section 22 of the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam, 2023** states that a confession made under inducement, threat, coercion, or promise (especially if it appears to give the accused a temporal advantage) is irrelevant in a criminal proceeding. However, if the impression caused by such inducement has been fully removed, the confession may become relevant.

**Conclusion**:

A confession to a police officer is **not admissible in court** as such. It can only be used if:

1. It is recorded by a Magistrate under **Section 183** of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023;
2. The Magistrate has explained that the accused is not bound to confess and that any confession may be used against him;
3. The confession is made voluntarily and the accused has been given a chance to refuse;
4. Only the specific fact discovered is admitted, as per **Section 23**.

Therefore, **confessions made directly to police officers are not admissible in court** unless properly recorded by a Magistrate in accordance with the law.

Your CCTV footage can be used in court as primary evidence under **Section 5 of the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam, 2023**, which deals with primary evidence of electronic records.

Specifically, **Section 5 of the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam, 2023** states:

> "Where an electronic or digital record is created or stored, and such storage occurs simultaneously or sequentially in multiple files, each such file is primary evidence."

This means that if your CCTV footage is stored in a digital format (e.g., on a hard drive, cloud storage, or a local server), and it was created and stored in a manner consistent with the normal operation of the system (such as continuous recording), **each file or version of the footage is primary evidence** of the events captured.

Additionally, **Section 63 of the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam, 2023** provides that electronic records (including video) are admissible in court **without further proof of the original**, provided the following conditions are satisfied:

1. The footage was produced by the CCTV system during a period when it was regularly used for monitoring activities (e.g., shop surveillance);
2. The system regularly received input (e.g., motion detection, time-stamped events) in the ordinary course of operation;
3. The system operated properly throughout the relevant period (or, if not, the malfunction did not affect the accuracy of the footage);
4. The footage reproduces or is derived from the actual events captured during that time.

To make your CCTV footage usable in court, you must:

- **Produce the footage in its original digital form** (e.g., from the CCTV device or storage unit);
- **Submit a certificate** (as required under **Section 63(4)**) that:
  - Identifies the electronic record (the footage);
  - Describes how it was produced (e.g., "recorded continuously from 10:00 AM to 11:30 AM on 5th April 2024");
  - Details the device involved (e.g., "CCTV camera installed at the entrance of the shop, model XYZ, connected to a local storage unit");
  - States that the footage was created during a period when the system was regularly used for surveillance;
  - Confirms that the system operated properly and that the footage accurately reflects the events;
  - Is signed by a person in charge of the CCTV system or the management of the shop (e.g., shop owner, manager, or security officer).

This certificate must be submitted **along with the footage** to the court.

Furthermore, **Section 530 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023** allows trials and proceedings to be conducted in electronic mode, including the recording of evidence through audio-video means. This means your CCTV footage can be played in court via electronic means, and the court may accept it as part of the evidence.

**Important Note**: While the footage is primary evidence, the court may still examine whether the footage is accurate, whether it was tampered with, or whether the recording was properly maintained. If there is any doubt about the authenticity or integrity of the footage, the court may require expert testimony or additional verification.

👉 **Recommendation**:  
You should preserve the original footage and ensure it is stored securely. Contact your local police station or the District Magistrate’s office to file a complaint and request that the footage be formally submitted as evidence. You may also seek assistance from a licensed advocate to prepare the certificate and present the footage in court.

